  "packet_sha256": "0a75572a8447b64b552a11b460acba9428259248a57d9125d9f1519a119bd2f2",
  "state_summary": "Round 1 reports 365 measured records, including all 32 previous acquisitions, and 2965 eligible candidates; no experiments are pending. Review of the supplied current records favors learning molecule-dependent and eluent-dependent endpoint responses. The new measurements strengthen the quinoline retention and injection-solvent questions, establish several localized prediction failures, and weaken simple loading or solvent-monotonicity explanations. Current predictions have changed substantially, so previous predictions should remain historical diagnostics.",
  "hypothesis_revisions": [
    {
      "id": "H_load",
      "status": "supported_with_substantial_revision",
      "observed_evidence": "m8 supports loading-dependent widening: at 1/1, 78.65, 157.3 and 235.95 mg give V1/V2 5.32/9.45, 4.23/11.45 and 4.22/13.04 mL. At 5/1, 78.65 versus 157.3 mg gives 5.74/11.29 versus 4.72/14.07. m92 changes direction with eluent: at 1/1 with EA loading, increasing 57.75 to 173.25 mg changes 5.18/12.01 to 6.29/9.73; at 50/1 it changes 22.57/52.44 to 20.51/62.21. At 10/1, the intermediate 115.5 mg result, 8.85/28.15, lies outside interpolation between the measured low and high loads.",
      "additional_evidence": "The m44 high-load repeat preserves earlier onset and later completion relative to the low-load record, but its onset differs from the first high-load measurement by 2.09 mL (61d5633c7221b3040a5f; 18d688363c7fa3da0609; 4d75c120ba1c4f0dae89). For m101 at 2/1, increasing approximately 50 to 150 mg moves both endpoints later by only 0.60/0.73 mL, unlike its stronger widening at 1/1.",
      "interpretation": "Endpoint-specific conditional responses are supported. A universal higher-load widening rule is rejected by m92 at 1/1. Adsorption saturation, dissolution and unrecorded variation remain competing hypotheses; the intermediate m92 result does not establish a smooth nonlinear loading law."
    },
    {
      "id": "H_ratio",
      "status": "split_into_distinct_local_questions",
      "observed_evidence": "m173 at fixed 124.8 mg and DCM 2000 uL has V2 108.89, 45.06 and 60.92 mL at 1/1, 2/1 and 5/1 (f90a5a5a54509c085ebd; 0b247863fd6e8aaf44d8; 1e775e7d8dcbf48fd6f8). Its completion response has a local minimum among these conditions. m73 gives 18.60/18.96 at 1/1, then 5.26/11.60 at 2/1, 5.63/13.55 at 5/1 and 9.59/28.68 at 50/1 at essentially identical mass and injection setup. Its unusual 1/1 result is localized relative to the subsequent progression.",
      "loading_control": "m80 at matched 105.5 mg gives 4.08/15.66 at 1/1 and 6.41/8.27 at 50/1 (55dfd30fde788076a355; 08155ffdcb0d27576050). The earlier completion at weaker eluent survives removal of the previous loading confound. The previous model predicted the new 50/1 measurement closely.",
      "interpretation": "These are recorded endpoint patterns, not established adsorption mechanisms. The m73 1/1 result especially warrants reproducibility assessment. General normal-phase solvent-strength expectations cannot justify replacing these labels with monotone values."
    },
    {
      "id": "H_onset",
      "status": "specific_question_resolved",
      "observed_evidence": "m177 at 10/1 and 140.6 mg measures 5.20/20.70 mL (ce14dd2481560a984aea), close to its matched 5/1 onset of 5.06 mL. The historical zero-onset prediction was a failure at that tested condition. m126 at 20/1 measures an ordered interval, 6.21/11.84 mL (87814eeffb35c15f3f89), contradicting its historical reversed prediction.",
      "interpretation": "These measurements support local prediction defects. They do not establish the cause of the defects or a universal endpoint correction."
    },
    {
      "id": "H_nitrogen",
      "status": "supported_for_quinolines_with_limited_transfer_claim",
      "observed_evidence": "At fixed loading, changing 1/1 to 5/1 moves methylquinoline m146 from 13.28/23.85 to 37.67/59.83 mL and quinoline m216 from 11.59/23.93 to 31.43/56.33. Historical 5/1 errors were -27.84/-32.47 and -20.35/-28.90 mL, respectively (04a2e8ad27ca641a3a12; 1ae3618ec277b0463686). In contrast, para-aminobenzonitrile m160 at 2/1 was predicted closely; its measured endpoints exceed the matched meta isomer by only 0.29/2.26 mL (2feb7d8e0a3566ddf97f; 53a1acff30433cff819f).",
      "chemical_knowledge": "Ring-nitrogen basicity, accessibility and interaction with silanols can affect silica retention. Aminobenzonitriles have a different electronic environment from quinolines.",
      "untested_hypothesis": "The substantial quinoline errors reflect insufficient graph-specific solvent response. Protonation or a particular silica binding geometry has not been demonstrated."
    },
    {
      "id": "H_injection",
      "status": "supported_for_one_solvent_identity_contrast_only",
      "observed_evidence": "For m92 at 1/1, 57.75 mg and a 200 uL plug, changing EA to PE loading moves 5.18/12.01 to 8.02/20.57 mL (00a89de0e6cc7377a84b; e626b5b8083ea1fd02f2): onset and completion increase by 2.84/8.56 mL. For m173 at 2/1, reducing DCM from 2000 to 1500 uL changes onset by -0.025 mL and completion by +1.16 mL, with a 0.05 mg mass difference (0b247863fd6e8aaf44d8; 9a32b65bc21fdc5f7286).",
      "interpretation": "Loading-solvent identity deserves further attention. The m173 pair supplies little evidence for a large plug-volume effect at 2/1. Both contrasts lack replication sufficient to establish general effects. Dissolution and injection focusing remain untested explanations."
    }
  ],
  "important_observations": [
    {
      "basis": "measured_feedback",
      "content": "The acetylenic diester m91 has much narrower measured intervals than historically predicted: 5.62/7.98 at 2/1 and 7.29/11.12 at 5/1 (d603d4af5fd62fa8b85e; a2ecf2b2f28094d70368). The historical model predicted onset too early and completion too late at both ratios. This differs from ethyl acetoacetate m82, whose 10/1 endpoints were both substantially underpredicted: measured 20.41/45.62 (6b8e53d2d8c8a90a7216). Shared carbonyl or acceptor counts do not explain these error directions."
    },
    {
      "basis": "measured_feedback_and_chemical_prior",
      "content": "Sulfoxide m199 measures 12.32/22.67 at 2/1, versus historical predictions 4.34/12.45 (b060fa3ae84f80e773d2). Strong interaction of a sulfoxide oxygen with silica is chemically plausible, but one measurement establishes an endpoint discrepancy, not its mechanism. Its supplied TPSA and HBA counts coincide with several ketones despite the different oxygen environment."
    },
    {
      "basis": "measured_feedback",
      "content": "At 5/1 with PE 300 uL loading, m60 measures 19.65/34.70 and benzylic alcohol m61 10.27/15.56 mL at near-matched masses (62d7ea74ab73fa6214b4; 994a1f65509549a84ede). The historical model underestimated m60 much more strongly. This supports investigating alcohol topology; chain length, hydroxyl placement and accessibility change together, so no single structural cause is isolated. Close cyclic-ketone transfer to m180 was comparatively accurate, and m181 completion was also predicted closely."
    },
    {
      "basis": "current_model_predictions",
      "content": "Loading sensitivity remains very small in current predictions. For m92 at 10/1, 57.75 versus 173.25 mg predicts 9.903/23.806 versus 9.905/23.814 mL (0389a6366b3a51fad8b5; ad0ace61c29817c678c2). These physical inputs already have measured labels. This is a training-fit sensitivity diagnostic, not new prospective accuracy evidence."
    }
  ],
